  3 of 21 recipe features survive conditioning on the agent: uses_rl, n_datasets, lr

                 feature       z  excess R2  share of the residual
                 uses_rl    +7.3    +0.0049   0.49%
              n_datasets    +4.4    +0.0173   1.73%
                      lr    +3.8    +0.0116   1.16%
  top_dataset has the largest excess (+0.1183) but +3.4 z on 394 levels -- it does not clear the bar, and a near-row-id never should on this test.

=== 4. split-half stability: does each survivor survive on half the rows? ===
  each half is ~587 rows and 13 families, so a real effect should reach about its full-data z over sqrt(2) -- that, not the z>3.5 bar, is the comparison

                 feature  full z  expected  observed  ratio    min   >bar
                 uses_rl    +7.3      +5.2      +3.4   0.65   +0.3   6/12
              n_datasets    +4.4      +3.1      +2.8   0.90   -0.0   2/12
                      lr    +3.8      +2.7      +1.7   0.62   +0.1   2/12

  holds up at >=0.85 of the attenuated expectation: n_datasets

  Read it as three sentences. (1) Something in the recipe is real: three
  features clear a Bonferroni bar against a permutation null, after the
  agent is conditioned out, and `peft` -- +0.1749 excess before
  conditioning, -0.0021 after -- shows what the conditioning is doing.
  (2) It is small: 0.5-1.7% of the residual variance each, against the
  66% agent identity takes on its own. (3) It is not sturdy: every
  survivor drops to near zero on at least one half, and only
  `n_datasets` attenuates no faster than halving the rows explains.
  1175 runs is about the smallest corpus that can see this at all, and
  there are no more runs to add. A predictor built on these features
  would be predicting the agent.
